Checkout failures with green hosts|CPU and memory look normal while customers cannot complete checkout.|sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="checkout",status=~"5.."}[5m]))|Define affected user operations and compare errors by region, version and dependency. Infrastructure utilization alone cannot establish service health.|Mitigate the failing path through a compatible rollback, traffic shift or feature disablement based on evidence. Establish a checkout SLI for ongoing detection.|Confirm successful end-to-end checkouts across affected cohorts and sustained recovery of the agreed SLI.
Fast error-budget burn|A service starts consuming its monthly error budget rapidly.|sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="api",status=~"5.."}[5m])) / sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="api"}[5m])) / 0.001|For the illustrative 99.9% SLO, compare short and long burn windows and verify traffic is nonzero. Identify the failure cohort and recent changes.|Apply the agreed incident and budget policy, reduce impact and pause risky rollout expansion. Do not wait for the monthly average to become visibly poor.|Verify both immediate user recovery and declining sustained burn, then assign reliability work from the incident evidence.
Retry storm blocks recovery|A dependency remains overloaded after the original fault is repaired.|sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="dependency"}[5m]))|Compare original user requests with downstream attempts, queue age and concurrent work. Multiple retry layers may sustain load beyond normal demand.|Reduce retry amplification, shed nonessential work and restore headroom before gradually reopening traffic.|Confirm attempts per original request, queue age and success rate stabilize without another synchronized retry surge.
Canary regression|A new version has slightly worse global metrics but severe errors for one client cohort.|kubectl rollout status deployment/APP -n NAMESPACE --timeout=60s|Compare canary and baseline populations by client, route and region. Check sample size and ensure the canary receives representative traffic.|Stop rollout and roll back or disable the affected feature if compatible. Correct canary analysis so important cohorts are not hidden by averages.|Verify the impacted cohort recovers and repeat a representative canary test before promotion.
Queue backlog threatens deadline|Background work grows older even though workers remain running.|max(queue_oldest_message_age_seconds{queue="orders"})|Compare arrival rate, completion rate, retry rate and dependency saturation. Determine whether additional workers would help or overload the bottleneck.|Throttle producers, prioritize urgent work or add measured capacity at the limiting stage. Bound retries and preserve duplicate-safe processing.|Confirm oldest-item age declines and representative jobs finish within the objective without downstream saturation.
Conflicting incident changes|Several responders are changing production simultaneously with no shared timeline.|journalctl -u APP.service --since '-15 min' --no-pager|Establish current impact and record active changes and owners. Separate uncertain hypotheses from observed evidence.|Assign incident coordination, pause conflicting changes and select one bounded mitigation at a time with a clear rollback condition.|Confirm responders share a timeline, each action has an owner and user impact is reducing before deeper diagnosis continues.
Backup exists but restore fails|A recovery drill discovers that database backups cannot produce a working application.|systemctl --failed --no-pager|Check backup integrity, database consistency, encryption keys, credentials and external dependencies. Measure how old the recoverable data actually is.|Repair the backup/restore procedure and missing dependencies, then test in isolation. Do not declare protection from backup-job success alone.|Complete representative transactions and document measured RTO/RPO against targets.
Noisy alert fatigue|On-call receives repeated pages that resolve without action.|up{job="api"}|Review incident outcomes, alert ownership and whether each page required a timely human action. Separate collection failures, symptoms and user-impact signals.|Remove or reroute nonactionable pages, add appropriate duration/grouping and retain actionable SLO or imminent-exhaustion alerts.|Replay historical conditions or rule tests to confirm real incidents still page promptly and benign fluctuations do not.
Capacity lost with one zone|A zone failure overloads the surviving instances.|kubectl get pods -n NAMESPACE -o wide|Compare surviving capacity, traffic distribution and dependency limits with the planned failure reserve. Scaling delay may exceed the impact tolerance.|Shed nonessential work and restore capacity in healthy zones using tested routing and scaling procedures. Avoid routing all traffic to an already saturated dependency.|Validate user SLIs and headroom under the failed-zone condition, then update capacity assumptions and exercises.
Automation expands an outage|An automated remediation repeatedly restarts healthy replicas during a dependency incident.|kubectl get events -n NAMESPACE --sort-by=.lastTimestamp|Inspect trigger conditions, scope and whether restarts improve the measured symptom. Dependency failures can make local health heuristics misleading.|Stop the harmful automation, restore serving capacity and redesign preconditions, concurrency bounds and stop rules.|Test dependency failures in a controlled environment and verify automation preserves capacity, stops on worsening signals and records actions.
